# Line endings are content in this repository.
#
# Every article is a text file whose bytes are the thing being published, and
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its system config. Without this
# file a fresh checkout on Windows rewrites all 615 of them as CRLF while
# `git status` stays clean, because git converts back on read - which is what
# makes it expensive to find rather than merely annoying.
#
# The reader tolerates it (ArticleParser normalises \r\n before it does
# anything else), so nothing would break loudly. It would simply mean the file
# an author edits and the file the build reads are different bytes, on some
# machines and not others - and the next thing that hashes an article would
# fail for no visible reason. The reader's own repository learned this from
# its seed digests; there is no reason to learn it twice.
#
# text=auto lets git normalise to LF on commit; eol=lf makes every checkout
# LF whatever the platform would otherwise do. Nothing tracked here is
# binary, so one rule covers all of it.

* text=auto eol=lf
